**Read:** [Mineralogy — Dexter Perkins, open editions](../sources/Mineralogy-Perkins.lingua#r_3Y3EJ93EG4V7Q0TZBCRRXVNVKP), the full crystallographic geometry/reciprocal-space material; [Crystallographic teaching pamphlets — IUCr](../sources/IUCr-Crystallographic-Teaching.lingua#r_334MQS5KEVRJ17XKK0WKCP6KWX), Wondratschek’s Matrices, mappings, and crystallographic symmetry in full; [Crystallography Open Database — structures, citations, and CIF records](../sources/Crystallography-Open-Database.lingua#r_0GWYXPDY9KYA4V4AXPCEFJG9RG), the selected structure’s cell/condition metadata and original reference.

Three cell lengths and three interaxial angles define the metric of a general unit cell. Fractional-coordinate distance is not generally the square root of the sum of squared fractional differences. Form the direct metric G from basis-vector dot products, so a displacement vector u has squared length uᵀGu.

The reciprocal basis describes plane spacings and diffraction geometry. State whether reciprocal vectors use the crystallographic convention or include a 2π factor; mixing conventions changes equations without changing physics. The inverse metric relates plane indices to spacing under the chosen definition.

For a cubic cell, d_hkl = a/√(h²+k²+l²). This simple formula relies on the cubic metric and cannot be reused unchanged for a monoclinic mineral. A basis transformation changes coordinates and index representations while preserving the physical distances/planes when performed consistently.

**Work:** derive the metric for orthogonal and one oblique cell, checking a distance against an independent Cartesian construction. Derive the cubic spacing formula from the reciprocal relation, then compare a general-cell calculation with a selected published structure. Explain how cell-parameter covariance could affect a derived spacing or distance.

Read the full crystallographic geometry and matrix pamphlet. Follow shared linear-algebra preparation where matrix inversion or basis changes are unfamiliar. The goal is to understand which geometry the calculation uses, rather than treating a structure viewer's numerical result as its own explanation.

**Continue:** [Use direction and plane indices with an explicit cell and convention](Directions-planes-and-crystallographic-indices.lingua#r_79PJ4B5E4TKFRY5SG9Y45YKTGA).

